    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Soul Drums by the Steve White Trio marks the prolific session drummer’s first album as a bandleader, serving as the inaugural release in Acid Jazz Records’ new Jazz Sessions series. Credited to White alongside Chris Hague (guitar) and Joel White (keyboards)—the duo known as Hague & White—the instrumental record is a cinematic tribute to White’s jazz roots, blending soul grooves with filmic influences. The album pays homage to early British, Hammond organ-led R&B from artists like Graham Bond and Brian Auger, while featuring guest saxophonist, horn player, and flutist Steve Beighton. Recorded spontaneously at Fox Den Studios in just two days, the project captures a raw, jazz-like energy with strong melodies and a distinct soulful feel that directly inspired the album’s title.\n\nThe nine-track collection includes a mellow, reimagined version of The Style Council’s classic “My Ever Changing Moods,” a fitting tribute to White’s fellow Councillors Paul Weller, Mick Talbot, and Dee C. Lee. The single “Changes” leads the album, which also features tracks like “Camera Obscura,” “Something,” and “When The Tourists Leave.” Released on March 20, 2026, Soul Drums is available on LP, CD, and digitally, with an Acid Jazz-exclusive “Opaque Spring Green” color vinyl edition limited to 500 copies. The album’s midcentury-inspired sleeve and its respectful nods to diverse genres reflect White’s desire to create a spontaneous, melodic work that honors the jazz and soul traditions that shaped his early career.",
